mdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;t6FPPaMJBpKttUICerW4mMYywibxOiP5PevTGjUBEXgƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx2026-07-21T14:42:59.091Zƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;y0pvpUcRL7sc2LSVDF_edYL6bRH_QAwBX1paE8PRMWgƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkxˆhttps://grist.org/global-indigenous-affairs-desk/as-climate-change-threatens-their-lands-indigenous-nations-demand-more-power-at-the-un/ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;-Vj_5M5ohEK5BDRSCLaJgHL9ivNwZqbBOjf8ghe9v4Iƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx9https://newscable-source-broker.invalid/activitypub/actorƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;zb4iK7RIQrhO7VAJxHOGUwVE0QQ3Iabye5DMJxW8fGQƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;XZb-xWgRGCTRwYnixDmv_0nw9gR-ZW6oAFcLiwysjtMƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkxˆhttps://grist.org/global-indigenous-affairs-desk/as-climate-change-threatens-their-lands-indigenous-nations-demand-more-power-at-the-un/ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;k22JR9CgqqqE3Wo5DlcTPg0ELtUEz2rCNTDpDUEMFnEƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarky#This story is published through the Indigenous News Alliance. The Arctic is warming four times faster than other regions, a trend that disproportionately harms the Indigenous SÃ¡mi people â€” particularly their access to vital traditional resources like reindeer and salmon, which are used for food, clothing, weapons, and more. As northern temperatures rise, snow is melting and refreezing at an accelerating rate. That forms ice, preventing reindeer from reaching the lichen below and dramatically increasing starvation rates . â€œThose impacts in nature are severe for our culture and livelihoods,â€ said Tuomas Aslak Juuso, vice president of the SÃ¡mediggi , or SÃ¡mi Parliament, of Finland. â€œSo survival of reindeer and then survival of our livelihoods [and] cultures is then questioned.â€ Last week, the impacts of climate change and colonialism were among the key issues raised by the hundreds of Indigenous people gathered in Geneva. Theyâ€™d come from around the world to discuss these mounting threats at the United Nations Expert Mechanism on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples, or EMRIP. They agreed that a multitude of crises will only grow worse unless they are given greater say in international negotiations and decision-making. Thatâ€™s why in addition to pushing specific policy recommendations on conflict, AI, and other key issues, Indigenous delegates continued their decades-long demand for better representation at the U.N. â€œWhat we are trying to do is to get some implementation resources for us to adapt to the climate change. With Finland, for example, we donâ€™t have any adaptation resources on how to adapt to these situations,â€ Juuso said. â€œTo be able to bring these issues forward to the global discussion, we would have to have the opportunity to participate.â€ Member states are granted access to U.N. gatherings, where they can easily express views, determine policies, and outline their progress implementing international commitments required by the United Nations Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples and other agreements. But Indigenous advocates say the voices of the peoples directly impacted by such actions arenâ€™t always heard at international forums.&nbsp; The U.N. structure requires participation through either a member state or a registered civil society agency â€” for instance, a non-governmental organization, academic institution, or accredited Indigenous organization. But Indigenous nations donâ€™t fit neatly into one of those categories and are reduced to participating as NGOs. Beyond those structural barriers, Indigenous peoples also face financial and other resource challenges that compound the difficulty of making themselves heard at the international level. Thatâ€™s why those attending EMRIP continued the long fight for what is called enhanced participation, which could finally give Indigenous nations and organizations the same access and power the United States and other countries enjoy. That increased influence is especially important when many countries ignore Indigenous priorities. Throughout the week, many delegates identified climate and environmental issues as one area where national governments often have different priorities than Indigenous peoples, who consider land and ecosystems more important than economic growth â€” a value system they believe is at odds with the worldâ€™s best interests.&nbsp; Read Next For Indigenous peoples, conflict means so much more than war Te Aniwaniwa Paterson â€œWeâ€™ve got to try and balance immediate economic interests with longer-term environmental protections,â€ said Kym Hamilton, who is from the tribes of NgÄti Kahungunu, NgÄ Rauru, NgÄti Raukawa in Aotearoa New Zealand. â€œI think weâ€™ve got a responsibility to protect our descendants.â€ In U.N. lexicon, enhanced participation is the catchphrase often used for efforts to elevate Indigenous peoplesâ€™ voices. Those gathered in Geneva asserted the right to represent their communities in crucial global discussions that impact them. They are also demanding more respect from member-state governments and other policymakers, something they argue will better help them to survive conflict and other crises. â€œThe question before us is no longer whether Indigenous peoples should participate more fully in the Human Rights Council,â€ Hamilton, who spoke on behalf of the National Iwi Chairs Forum that represents 87 tribes, said. â€œThe question now is how quickly we will implement these commitments.â€&nbsp; She offered recommendations for EMRIPâ€™s seven expert Indigenous members, including a clear timeline for creating participatory status for Indigenous peoplesâ€™ institutions and appointing Indigenous and state representatives with equal standing to lead the process. She also called for the establishment of an independent and transparent accreditation mechanism to prevent states from vetoing Indigenous representatives, and initiatives to provide predictable and sustainable funding to take part. Hamilton said Indigenous peoplesâ€™ full participation in international deliberations includes the right to decide who represents their communities. â€œGovernments donâ€™t always tell the truth,â€ she said, citing the U.N. Human Rights Councilâ€™s Universal Periodic Review â€” which all 193 member states must undergo every 4.5 years â€” which she said revealed gaps between the reports governments submitted and the actual human rights situations within their jurisdictions. Kenneth Deer, member of the Mohawk Nation of KahnawÃ :ke and longtime international Indigenous advocate, echoed that point. â€œThe government never speaks ill about itself,â€ he said. â€œYou canâ€™t let government speak for us.â€ But the U.N.â€™s accreditation and recognition process can hinder progress toward collectively tackling the challenges impacting Indigenous peoples, he noted. â€œFor us, as a representative institution, there isnâ€™t actually any clear path to accreditate ourselves â€” and that leads to them trying to improvise, and try to find ways how we can accreditate ourselves.â€ Read Next Indigenous cultural practices are a climate solution, report finds Joseph Lee The National Congress of American Indians also called for increased Indigenous participation at the international level. â€œRecognition and participation of Indigenous peoples must accommodate the numerous ways we identify, organize, and represent ourselves,â€ President Mark Macarro said. â€œIt is essential that we participate directly in matters of global concern that affect our environment, health, human rights, and unique rights as Indigenous peoples.â€ Deer said heâ€™s seen many caucuses of Indigenous delegates at the U.N. engage in important collaborations. But he wishes they could have a higher profile. â€œIndigenous peoples want to have a voice,â€ he said. â€œThey want to be heard.â€ A diversity of voices at the U.N.â€™s highest levels, he believes, would improve the U.N. processes and system to the benefit of all countries. â€œWhat we need is the states, now, to agree to a process that was run by Indigenous people,â€ he said. Throughout the week, EMRIP delegates identified a host of other barriers to participation. For example, communications technology within remote communities presents challenges to joining global deliberations. Additionally, there are significant financial barriers for under-resourced communities when U.N. forums are held far from their homelands. The U.N. Voluntary Fund for Indigenous Peoples , which helps finance travel to U.N. forums, is also experiencing budget shortfalls, inhibiting many delegatesâ€™ ability to attend. It is seen as an important way to increase participation among delegates from areas where governments do not recognize the Indigenous peoples within their borders. Sheryl Lightfoot, EMRIPâ€™s North America region member, is an Anishinaabe expert in Indigenous rights, global politics, and public policy. She said states are not involved in how EMRIP officially recognizes Indigenous peoples. â€œWorldwide, thereâ€™s a need â€” and this is especially true across Africa and Asia â€” of Indigenous peoples seeking just mere recognition of their existence from their governments,â€ she said. â€œStates seem to be more resistant now than they were 10 years ago to recognize that peoples are Indigenous peoples and have those rights attached to them.â€ Despite Hamiltonâ€™s doubts about how much change has been achieved internationally, hearing her peers from other Indigenous nations testify about how theyâ€™ve persevered inspired her and restored her hope. â€œI feel like thereâ€™s a real sense of â€¦ urgency and concern,â€ she said. â€œWe donâ€™t want our grandchildrenâ€™s grandchildren to have to keep coming here and doing this stuff.â€ This story was originally published by Grist with the headline As climate change threatens their lands, Indigenous nations demand more power at the UN on Jul 21, 2026.ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;44k7rbCIbFlhBG1J3TNGjz5cMVi0CyOBcbqBOQFMdGoƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx9ni:///sha-256;hIOhTJe6N1K7bDeohPWIJGlg6pOBTnuavqCCj4Z0vJoƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;OiS9FkNRg5IzXTwBnyPx8vwEsoQrTeujtjVIkeSBubwƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;z6dYFsMvH23QriAdkvEtu2CNtxesIO3nslKICCLfbAEƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkefalseƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;898OD3wE1UE0N6ipRmbosvt2Hl95cb7-gcK-2YqJ6gEƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkdtrueƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;39HvAXfJonCgQwRTXsWyUGbHYSlfEshnVc_6CPuIyZgƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;CtRAFDRKPOmLN9FlJ8R8ViAvKMsoJ7cZSCE9337z2dsƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkdTextƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;TX64RPWJpucvlSLPWpEFIlTaByZAcTLj7SOamYC10r0ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkqSyndicationSourceƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEnti